ws. Probably better to aim for standard hand: keep one pair and form chows/pungs from others.\n\nIf we aim for standard hand, we need to break three pairs to form melds. That means we should consider which pairs are easiest to convert to pungs/chows. W3: could pung if we draw third (3 left). T8: could pung (3 left) or chow with T6,T7 or T7,T9. B7: could pung (2 left) or chow with B6,B8 or B8,B9. B9: could pung (3 left) or chow with B7,B8.\n\nGiven available tiles, B8 is abundant (4), so B7,B8,B9 chow is possible if we break B7 and B9 pairs. That could yield two chows? Actually, we have two B7 and two B9. If we get two B8, we could form two chows: [B7,B8,B9] and another [B7,B8,B9] using both B7 and B9. That would use both pairs. But we need four melds total; we already have one meld (W3-W5). So we need three more. Possibly two chows from B7/B9/B8 and one more from T tiles.\n\nThus, keeping B7 and B9 pairs is good for potential chows. T8 pair could be used for chow with T6,T7 or pung. W3 pair could be pung.\n\nDiscard among T7, T3, T4. T7 is less connected to other tiles (only connects to T8 via T6). T3 and T4 connect to each other and to T2/T5. So T3 and T4 are more valuable for chow formation. Therefore, discard T7 is best to maintain flexibility and keep chow options open.\n\nAlso, from history, previous player played T7 recently (last action: Play T7). That might indicate T7 is safe to discard (less likely to be needed by others for pung). But we don't know.\n\nGiven valid actions, discard T7 seems optimal.\n\n**Final Choice: Play T7**\n\nEvaluation: Discard T7 preserves pairs and keeps chow potential with T3-T4 and B7-B9 combinations.\nReason: T7 has limited chow potential due to T6 abundance but requires breaking T8 pair; T3 and T4 offer better chow options with available T2/T5. Maintaining multiple pairs provides flexibility for both standard hand and Seven Pairs possibilities.\nAnswer: Play T7"}
